{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch7Kdu4GxoTFGCGKax4MR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Before I draft, a few questions. The answers will change what the summary can honestly claim:\n\n1. **The $33,000 gap each year.** The attorney costs $78,000 a year and the foundation caps awards at $45,000. Where does the rest come from? Is it the county's $60,000, another grant, or something you're still looking for? The foundation will spot that gap right away, so the summary should say how you'll close it.\n\n2. **The attorney who leaves in June.** Will you replace them with other money, so this grant really adds a third lawyer? Or would this hire partly fill that seat? If the grant mostly keeps you at two attorneys, the summary has to make a different case: losing capacity instead of adding it.\n\n3. **What one more attorney would deliver.** At about 80 open cases each, how many more tenants a year would a third lawyer represent? Would they cover something your current team can't, like a particular courthouse, an eviction docket day, or a language? I want a number you're willing to commit to, along with your start date and grant period.\n\n4. **The October county renewal.** How likely is it to renew at $60,000? Is anything uncertain, like budget cuts or a new board? If the foundation is likely to ask, it may be better to address it directly.\n\n5. **Your record.** How long has the clinic been running? Has it handled grants like this before, or had a judge, the court, or a funder praise its work? Even one line would help show you can deliver.\n\n6. **Anything the foundation knows or will find out.** Is there a weak number, a cost, or a past problem I haven't heard about? Examples: an earlier grant that fell short, hiring trouble, or the 58% including cases that might have turned out fine without a lawyer.\n\nIf you don't know some of these, just say so. I'll write around those gaps and won't guess."}],